Hi [~accountid:5bb1cc64c9dad04734ea835a] and all

Sorry for a delay this week, had to prioritise other matters. I had a look at the DPA by Apollo, and here are my thoughts:

Overall, not as bad as I expected. Still there are some issues:

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Clause 4.2.*

The mechanism for notification of a proposed update to the sub-processor list is not compliant with the GDPR.

_“We will provide Notice by either (a) posting such Subprocessors at the following webpage:_ [_https://www.apollo.io/company/privacy-center_|https://www.apollo.io/company/privacy-center] _or_ [_https://trust.apollo.io/_|https://trust.apollo.io/]_,_ {color:#bf2600}*_or_*{color} _(b) sending an email to the last known email address.”_

To effectively notify Bolt, posting Subprocessors list on a webpage would not be enough - Bolt should actively be notified. The text should read “and”, instead of “or”.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Exhibit 1A (B).*

With my understanding of the service, its not clear why the following categories of data will have to be processed by Apollo (including e.g., Bolt employees):

* *Video and audio recordings*

This must not be in the scope of the service. 
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Clause. 1.2.*

Security Incident Notification - 72 hours currently indicated, whereas we would like this to be 48 hours max according to our internal guidelines.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Clause 5.5.*

_“Audits or inspections should not be conducted more than once per year (unless requested by a Supervisory Authority).”_

We would prefer not to accept that as such limitations as per our internal guidelines.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Liability* 

Liability in the DPA references the ToS. Re/ the liability clause of the ToS:

I see there is a liability cap (12 months worth of service fees). To confirm my previous point, as per our internal guidelines, we cannot agree to a liability cap given the possible sanctions under the GDPR.

However, considering the risks of the arrangement, in my personal view the cap could be accepted, as the fees paid to Apollo should be substantial enough to cover us in case of a breach from their side - due to the limited scope of data they will factually process.

I would push for the removal of the liability cap, but if unsuccessful, I believe we could accept it.
{panel}

*As for our controllership obligations*, overall, I did not spot any reflags.

