No PR was open, only did analysis and it can be fixed via two options:

# Quick Fix:
## To extend existing function {{processError}} and add {{API Error Code 503}} and return desired error code.
#*For example:*
{noformat}public static processError(ctx: Context, logger: Logger, e: any, errorData: ErrorData): BoltError {
        if (e instanceof BoltError && e.getErrorResponse().error_hint === "Timeout") {
            logger.warning(ctx, "timeout", {errorData: errorData});
            return BoltError.code(CommonResponseCodes.CLIENT_TIMEOUT);
        } else if (
            e instanceof BoltError &&
            (e.getErrorResponse().error_hint?.toLowerCase().includes("socket hang up") ||
                e.getErrorResponse().error_hint?.toLowerCase().includes("econnreset"))
        ) {
            logger.warning(ctx, "socket hang up", {errorData: errorData});
            return BoltError.code(PaymentResponseCodes.SOCKET_HANG_UP);
        } else if (
            e instanceof BoltError &&
            (e.getErrorResponse().error_hint?.toLowerCase().includes("API Error Code=503"))
        ) {
            logger.warning(ctx, "API Error Code=503", {errorData: errorData});
            return BoltError.code(PaymentResponseCodes.PAYMENT_ERROR_TECHNICAL);
        } else {
            logger.error(ctx, errorData.error_message ?? "unknown", {errorData: errorData});
            return BoltError.code(errorData.error_code, errorData.error_message);
        }
    }{noformat}
# Better Fix (for need more time to implement and then do the analysis of returned response for such cases):
## Extend {{ProcessOutExternalApiParser}} and add override for {{decodeError}} which will return formatted response.
Example by [~accountid:5fc10e1ffacfd6007606bd5b] 
{noformat}class ProcessOutExternalApiParser<T extends ProcessOutSuccessResponse> extends ExternalRequestClientParserJson<
    object,
    T,
    ProcessOutErrorResponse
> {

    public decodeError(ctx: Context, errorData: Buffer): ResponseObject<ProcessOutErrorResponse> {
        try {
            return super.decodeError(ctx, errorData);
        } catch (e) {
            return Response.data({success: false, error_type: "unparsed.json.error", message: errorData.toString()})
        }
    }

    /**
     * Synthetic method for casting to strong typed parser. Actually we used one
     * "common" parser instance for all API calls. To make it strong typed, just
     * use `this.parser.typed()` instead of `this.parser`, to compute types automatically.
     */
    public typed<R extends ProcessOutSuccessResponse>() {
        return this as unknown as ProcessOutExternalApiParser<R>;
    }
}{noformat}

I just called him and tried to explain our system limitations and I insisted on the fact that we don’t refuse to cooperate and we want to help. He was calm, he said he understands GDPR law but he said:
- I requested the data on the 8th of December and requested to be provided before the 15 of December;
- I appreciate the fact you searched for that specific phone number, but with no results, we need all the trips for the whole year;
*- I sent you a new request to provide until today EOD the name of the legal representative and we want it >> I told him I would get back to him with an answer today by 12:00 tonight ;*
-I provided all the necessary details in the original request, we need all the trips for 1 year to be able to determine who is involved and who is not in the investigation;


I tried to explain to him that our system doesn't allow us to find the trips in an easy way and if we provide all the trips requested, we will need a lot more time ( as his original request stated that we had 7 days to prepare and send the response).

So, I would say he is pretty determined to issue a fine for us and he insisted on offering him the name of the legal representative TODAY. 

What should we do?
[~accountid:62d7b457807cf6f78ca9d117] [~accountid:641d70192cee496759ec78f0] [~accountid:60f2a71843c956007065f2ad]

I would say that we should respond in written with the following:

* unfortunately, we cannot provide these data in the requested time;
* we need more information to do this internal investigation: according to what was requested, we have no way to identify and provide the data. It’s in the interest of both parties that we can collaborate and support the investigation, for the benefit of the Police;
* the data requested do not belong to us, they are our client’s data, therefore we are obliged by the Data Protection Authority to comply with GDPR;
* The company data is public.

